.\" Not a v7 page: v7 had no directory library at all.  See BESM-6 NOTES.
.TH DIRECTORY 3
.SH NAME
opendir, readdir, closedir, rewinddir, telldir, seekdir, dirfd
\- read a directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <dirent.h>
.PP
.B DIR *opendir(const char *name);
.PP
.B struct dirent *readdir(DIR *dirp);
.PP
.B int closedir(DIR *dirp);
.PP
.B void rewinddir(DIR *dirp);
.PP
.B long telldir(DIR *dirp);
.PP
.B void seekdir(DIR *dirp, long loc);
.PP
.B int dirfd(DIR *dirp);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Opendir
opens the directory named by
.I name
and returns a pointer to a directory stream, positioned at the first entry.  It
returns NULL if the name cannot be opened, or is not a directory, or if there is
no memory for the stream.
.PP
.I Readdir
returns a pointer to the next entry, or NULL at the end of the directory.  The
object it points at has the following structure:
.RS
.PP
.nf
struct dirent {
    ino_t d_ino;
    int   d_namlen;
    char  d_name[DIRSIZ + 1];
};
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B d_ino
is the entry's i-number and is never zero;
.B d_name
is the name, NUL-terminated;
.B d_namlen
is its length, which
.I readdir
has already computed, so a caller need not call
.IR strlen (3).
.PP
The structure is the stream's own and the next
.I readdir
on the same stream overwrites it, exactly as
.IR getpwent (3)
overwrites its own.  A caller that wants to keep a name copies it out.
.PP
.I Closedir
closes the stream, releases everything
.I opendir
allocated, and returns the result of the underlying
.IR close (2).
.PP
.I Telldir
returns a value naming the position
.I readdir
will read from next;
.I seekdir
returns the stream to a position
.I telldir
reported earlier.
.I Rewinddir
returns it to the beginning.
.PP
.I Dirfd
returns the descriptor the stream is holding, so that a caller may
.IR fstat (2)
the directory it is walking without opening it a second time.  A caller must not
close, read or seek that descriptor: the stream's buffer and position would then
describe a place in the file that no longer exists.
.SH FILES
.nf
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
lib/libc/gen/opendir.c
lib/libc/gen/readdir.c
lib/libc/gen/closedir.c
lib/libc/gen/rewinddir.c
lib/libc/gen/telldir.c
lib/libc/gen/seekdir.c
lib/libc/gen/dirfd.c
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
dir(5), open(2), read(2), stat(2), ttyname(3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Opendir
returns NULL and sets
.I errno:
.B ENOTDIR
when the name is not a directory, and otherwise whatever
.IR open (2)
or
.IR malloc (3)
set.
.I Readdir
returns NULL at the end of the directory and on a read error alike, and does not
distinguish them.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.B This is not a v7 interface.
v7 had none at all: every caller opened the directory as a file and
.IR read (2)
raw
.B struct direct
records out of it, and eleven programs in
.I cmd/
each carried their own copy of the same mistakes.  4.2BSD introduced the library
and this is its shape, less what has no meaning here.
.PP
.B struct dirent is not struct direct.
.IR dir (5)'s
.B struct direct
is the format on the disk: exactly four words, with
.B d_name
being
.B DIRSIZ
characters and
.B no room for a terminator
when a name fills the field.
.B DIRSIZ is 18 here,
not v7's 14.
.I Readdir
copies the name into a field one character longer and plants that terminator,
which is the whole reason for the library: a
.IR strlen (3)
on the on-disk field runs into the next entry's
.BR d_ino .
A
.B struct dirent
is never read into and nothing about it is on any disk.
.PP
.B readdir skips the empty slots.
.IR unlink (2)
empties a directory entry by writing
.B d_ino
= 0 back in place, so a hole sits in the middle of a live directory and every
open-coded caller in this tree tests for it by hand.
.I Readdir
does that test once, for all of them.
.PP
.B telldir returns the plain byte offset,
and there is no machinery behind it.  4.2BSD returns an index into a list of seek
points that
.I closedir
must free, because a BSD directory entry is variable-length and an offset alone
cannot name one.  Every entry here is exactly
.B DIRENTSZ
(24) bytes, so the offset is the entry.
.I Seekdir
rounds its argument down to a whole entry.
.PP
.B The read buffer is sized from the directory
\- as many entries as it holds when it is opened, capped at
.B DIRPB
(128), which is exactly one filesystem block, so a full read is one buffer-cache
block and no entry straddles the buffer.  A fixed one-block buffer would be 512
words for a
.I /dev
of twenty-five entries, and
.B _NFILE
is 20: seventeen directories open at once would be 8,704 words of a 28,672-word
address space.
.PP
.B opendir refuses a plain file,
which neither v7 nor 4.2BSD had to do.
.IR Read (2)
on a regular file succeeds here, so without the test
.I readdir
would hand back file contents reinterpreted as entries \- silently, there being no
field in a directory entry that can be checked for sense.
.PP
.B lib/test/dirt is the test, and it runs on the disk image only.
.I b6sim
serves every system call from the host, where
.IR open (2)
and
.IR fstat (2)
on a directory both
.B succeed
and only
.IR read (2)
refuses.  So under the simulator
.I opendir
returns a perfectly good stream and the first
.I readdir
returns NULL: every directory looks empty, and looks it in exactly the way an
empty one does.  No expectation file can tell those apart, which is why the test
is image-only and why a
.I b6sim
run of anything that walks a tree proves nothing about the walk.
